(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["kena"]={"slug":"kena","name":"Kena","deva":"केन","note":"","intro":["“By whom?” — the questioning Upaniṣad<br>All four khaṇḍas — Sāma-Veda, Talavakāra/Jaiminīya recension<br>Devanāgarī · IAST · Pada-by-Pada Gloss · Literal · Paraphrase · Five Commentaries","Sanskrit text courtesy of sanskritdocuments.org (Talavakāra/Jaiminīya-Brāhmaṇa recension).<br>English glosses cross-checked against Śaṅkara’s Pada-bhāṣya and Vākya-bhāṣya<br>(Gambhirananda tr., Advaita Ashrama), Sri Aurobindo’s Kena Upanishad (1914, rev. 1952),<br>Sivananda’s The Principal Upanishads, Krishnananda’s Kena-Upaniṣad lectures,<br>and Paul Deussen’s Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda (1897)."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"1. About the Kena Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Kena Upaniṣad — also called the Talavakāra Upaniṣad after the Sāma-Veda branch (śākhā) in which it is preserved — belongs to the ninth adhyāya of the Talavakāra (Jaiminīya) Brāhmaṇa of the Sāma-Veda. Of the ten principal Upaniṣads it is the only one whose host text is a Sāma-Veda Brāhmaṇa, and the only one consisting of two structurally distinct halves: khaṇḍas 1–2 are metrical verse, khaṇḍas 3–4 are narrative prose.","The text takes its conventional name from its opening word, ‘kena’ — ‘by whom?’. The whole of khaṇḍa 1 is a chain of such questions: by whom willed does the mind descend on its objects? by whom yoked does the breath stir? by whom uttered is speech? by whom does the eye see, the ear hear? The answers, in khaṇḍa 1 itself, are not given as positive identifications but as via negativa: that which neither speech utters, nor mind thinks, nor eye sees, nor ear hears, nor breath breathes — that is Brahman, not the deity worshipped here.","Khaṇḍa 2 deepens the via negativa into the most striking single utterance of the Upaniṣadic corpus: ‘I do not think I know it well; nor do I think I do not know it. He among us who knows it, knows it; nor does he not know.’ This is the canonical four-cornered logic (catuṣ-koṭi) of Brahman-knowledge, which the later Mādhyamika takes up.","Khaṇḍas 3–4, in prose, switch to narrative. After a victory of the gods (devāḥ) over the demons (asurāḥ), a Yakṣa-form appears — Brahman in disguise. Agni and Vāyu come to it, are challenged ‘who are you, and what can you do?’, fail, and return defeated. Indra goes; the Yakṣa vanishes; in the same space (ākāśa) Indra finds a woman, Umā Haimavatī, who tells him: that was Brahman, by whose victory you were rejoicing. The Upaniṣad then closes with a series of short aphorisms — the lightning-glimpse, the inner-Self version, the seat of meditation (tad vanam) — and seals itself with the famous triad: ‘tapo damaḥ karmeti pratiṣṭhā.’","The Kena is therefore the principal Upaniṣad of the question, of the dialogical method, and of the demonstration that to claim to know Brahman is precisely not to know it. It is also the principal Upaniṣadic source for the doctrine that the inner senses (the mind behind the mind, the breath behind the breath) are Brahman’s functioning."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. Manuscript Variations and Numbering","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Kena has a stable text — no recensional divergence comparable to the Mādhyandina / Kāṇva split of the Īśā. What does vary across editions is the numbering of the prose sections in khaṇḍas 3–4: some print 12 + 9 sections, some 11 + 9, some run khaṇḍa 4 to 8. The most widely cited numbering today is Gambhirananda’s (the one used by Advaita Ashrama), which is also the one Śaṅkara’s Vākya-bhāṣya follows. This is the numbering used below.","<table><tr><td>Edition</td><td>Total sections</td><td>Veda affiliation</td><td>Notes</td></tr><tr><td>Talavakāra-Brāhmaṇa (Sāma-Veda, embedded text)</td><td>≈ 35 (4 khaṇḍas)</td><td>Sāma-Veda</td><td>Host text; the Upaniṣad is JaiminīyaUpaniṣad-Brāhmaṇa 4.18 ff.</td></tr><tr><td>Śaṅkara, Pada-bhāṣya + Vākya-bhāṣya</td><td>4 khaṇḍas (verse 1–2 + prose 3–4)</td><td>Sāma-Veda</td><td>Pada-bhāṣya covers the verse khaṇḍas word-by-word; Vākya-bhāṣya covers the prose. The standard classical reading.</td></tr><tr><td>Gambhirananda (Advaita Ashrama), Eight Upaniṣads vol. 1</td><td>4 khaṇḍas (8 + 5 + 12 + 9)</td><td>Sāma-Veda</td><td>Standard modern English; follows Śaṅkara’s numbering. Used here.</td></tr><tr><td>Hume (1921), The Thirteen Principal Upanishads</td><td>4 khaṇḍas (8 + 5 + 12 + 9)</td><td>Sāma-Veda</td><td>Same numbering; literal translation.</td></tr><tr><td>Deussen (1897), Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda</td><td>4 khaṇḍas (8 + 5 + 11 + 9)</td><td>Sāma-Veda</td><td>Merges Gambhirananda’s 3.11–3.12 (Indra’s approach + the Yakṣa’s vanishing) into a single section; otherwise identical.</td></tr><tr><td>Olivelle (1998), Early Upaniṣads</td><td>4 khaṇḍas, prose sections re-numbered</td><td>Sāma-Veda</td><td>Critical edition; argues that 4.7–4.9 are a later coda.</td></tr><tr><td>Sri Aurobindo (1914), Kena Upanishad</td><td>4 khaṇḍas (8 + 5 + 12 + 9)</td><td>Sāma-Veda</td><td>Follows standard numbering; the most substantial single English commentary on the Kena.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. The Five Commentaries Summarised","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The same stack used in the Īśāvāsya, calibrated to the Kena. Of these five, Aurobindo’s Kena commentary is the longest he wrote on any single Upaniṣad and was published in his lifetime under his own corrections — it is arguably his deepest philosophical text. Śaṅkara wrote two separate bhāṣyas on the Kena (Pada- and Vākya-), the only Upaniṣad on which he did so.","Swami Gambhirananda (Advaita Ashrama, Eight Upaniṣads with the Commentary of Śaṅkarācārya, vol. 1, 1957) — Carrier of Śaṅkara’s Pada-bhāṣya (on khaṇḍas 1–2) and Vākya-bhāṣya (on khaṇḍas 3–4) in English. Śaṅkara reads the Upaniṣad as the canonical proof-text for the doctrine that the inner Brahman is not a graspable object of any sense or faculty — its functioning is the very functioning of the faculties, never their object.","Swami Sivananda (Divine Life Society, The Principal Upanishads, 1937; Kena lectures) — Practical sādhanā-Advaita. Frames the ‘by whom?’ inquiry as a daily morning practice; reads khaṇḍa 2 as the canonical Upaniṣadic refutation of intellectual arrogance; the Yakṣa story as a parable of the ego’s ‘who am I to be challenged?’ collapsing before the inner divine.","Swami Krishnananda (Divine Life Society, The Kena Upaniṣad, 1971; Rishikesh lectures) — Philosophical Advaita. Krishnananda is unusually attentive to the structural logic of the four-cornered formula in 2.3, treating it as the Upaniṣadic prototype of the catuṣ-koṭi that the Mādhyamika later develops. Reads the Yakṣa story as an epistemological allegory: the gods are the senses/faculties, the Yakṣa is the Self that lies behind them; the senses cannot grasp their own source.","Paul Deussen (1897, Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda) — Indological-historical reading. Treats the verse half (khaṇḍas 1–2) and prose half (khaṇḍas 3–4) as originally independent texts that were joined in late Brāhmaṇa redaction; gives a full philological treatment of vana / tadvanam in 4.6 as the Upaniṣadic prototype of the later cit-prema doctrine. Translates yakṣa as ‘mysteriöse Wesen’ — mysterious being — and warns against identifying it with later Yakṣa-iconography.","Sri Aurobindo (1914, rev. 1952, Kena Upanishad) — Modern integral reading. The longest of his Upaniṣadic commentaries. Aurobindo reads the four khaṇḍas as a single unbroken argument: khaṇḍa 1 asks the question; khaṇḍa 2 deepens it into the four-cornered impossibility of any ordinary knowing of Brahman; khaṇḍa 3 dramatises the same point by showing the cosmic gods (Agni, Vāyu, even Indra) reduced to ignorance before the Yakṣa; khaṇḍa 4 resolves it through the feminine principle Umā as the revealer. The Kena, for Aurobindo, is the Upaniṣad of consecration of knowledge to that-which-knows-through-it.","Order of commentary digests below: Gambhirananda (Śaṅkara) — Sivananda — Krishnananda — Deussen — Aurobindo."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Peace Invocation (Sāma-Veda Śānti-pāṭha)","title":"","sa":["ॐ आप्यायन्तु ममाङ्गानि वाक्प्राणश्चक्षुः श्रोत्रमथो बलमिन्द्रियाणि च सर्वाणि ।","सर्वं ब्रह्मौपनिषदं माहं ब्रह्म निराकुर्यां मा मा ब्रह्म निराकरोत् ।","अनिराकरणमस्त्वनिराकरणं मेऽस्तु ।","तदात्मनि निरते य उपनिषत्सु धर्मास्ते मयि सन्तु ते मयि सन्तु ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ āpyāyantu mamāṅgāni vāk prāṇaś cakṣuḥ śrotram atho balam indriyāṇi ca sarvāṇi |","sarvaṃ brahmaupaniṣadaṃ māhaṃ brahma nirākuryāṃ mā mā brahma nirākarot |","anirākaraṇam astv anirākaraṇaṃ me ’stu |","tad ātmani nirate ya upaniṣatsu dharmās te mayi santu te mayi santu ||","oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"“Oṃ. May my limbs, speech, breath, eye, ear, and also strength and all the senses be nourished. All is the Brahman of the Upaniṣads. May I not deny Brahman; may Brahman not deny me. Let there be no denial; let there be no denial from me. May the dharmas of the Upaniṣads be in me — in me, delighting in the Self. Oṃ. Peace, peace, peace.”","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This is the canonical Sāma-Veda śānti-mantra and is shared with the Chāndogya. It is doctrinally heavier than the Śukla-Yajurveda ‘pūrṇam adaḥ’: it does not assert the Full but asks for the senses to be nourished (āpyāyantu) and pledges a non-denial pact between the seeker and Brahman. The Kena, an Upaniṣad of the question and the failed claim, opens not with a metaphysical statement but with a request and a pact. That is the right key for the four khaṇḍas that follow."]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"The Four Khaṇḍas","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Verses (khaṇḍas 1–2) are given as Devanāgarī → IAST → pada-by-pada gloss → literal → paraphrase → five-commentary digest. Prose units (khaṇḍas 3–4) are hybrid: pivotal dialogue and aphorisms are pad-glossed; narrative connective tissue is rendered as flowing prose.","Grammar abbreviations: m./f./n.; nom./acc./instr./dat./abl./gen./loc./voc.; sg./du./pl.; pres./impf./pf./pot./impv./ger./inf./caus.; pp. = past passive participle; pap. = present active participle."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Khaṇḍa 1 — The “By Whom?” inquiry (verse, 8 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Mantra 1.1","title":"By whom willed does the mind descend?","sa":["ॐ केनेषितं पतति प्रेषितं मनः केन प्राणः प्रथमः प्रैति युक्तः ।","केनेषितां वाचमिमां वदन्ति चक्षुः श्रोत्रं क उ देवो युनक्ति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["oṃ keneṣitaṃ patati preṣitaṃ manaḥ kena prāṇaḥ prathamaḥ praiti yuktaḥ |","keneṣitāṃ vācam imāṃ vadanti cakṣuḥ śrotraṃ ka u devo yunakti || 1 ||"],"words":[["kena","pron. m. instr. sg.","by whom?"],["iṣitam","n. nom. sg. pp. of √iṣ","willed / sent"],["patati","3 sg. pres. of √pat","descends / falls (i.e., alights on its object)"],["preṣitam","n. nom. sg. pp. of pra-√iṣ","impelled forth"],["manaḥ","n. nom. sg.","the mind"],["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the breath / vital force"],["prathamaḥ","m. nom. sg. adj.","first / foremost"],["praiti","3 sg. pres. of pra-√i","goes forth / proceeds"],["yuktaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp. of √yuj","yoked / employed"],["vācam","f. acc. sg.","speech"],["imām","pron. f. acc. sg.","this"],["vadanti","3 pl. pres. of √vad","they utter"],["cakṣuḥ","n. acc. sg.","the eye"],["śrotram","n. acc. sg.","the ear"],["kaḥ u devaḥ","pron. m. nom. sg. + emph. + m. nom. sg.","what god, indeed"],["yunakti","3 sg. pres. of √yuj","yokes / commissions"]],"lit":"“Oṃ. By whom willed does the impelled mind descend (on its object)? By whom yoked does the foremost breath proceed? By whom willed do they utter this speech? What god is it that commissions the eye and the ear?”","para":"By whose impulse does the mind alight on its objects? Who yokes the breath into its activity? Who utters speech through the speaker? Who commissions the eye to see and the ear to hear?","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘kena iṣitam’ — the question is about the cause that itself is conscious; the question presupposes that the senses do not move on their own, but are moved. ‘kaḥ u devaḥ’ — ‘what god?’ — is a deliberate puzzle: it is no devata in the cosmic-pantheon sense; the Upaniṣad will reveal it as the Self that lights every faculty."],["Sivananda","The single most fertile question in the Upaniṣadic corpus, and the right opening for a day’s sādhanā: pause before any sense-act and ask, ‘by whom is this happening?’"],["Krishnananda","The verb ‘patati’ — descends — is deliberate. Mind does not ‘go to’ an object as an equal; it falls upon it, dropping from above. This is the Upaniṣad’s first hint that mind is itself a derivative function of a higher power."],["Deussen","Compares with Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 3.7 (the antaryāmin-brāhmaṇa). The Kena’s ‘kena’ is the metaphysical question Yājñavalkya answers there in the language of the inner controller; the Kena will answer it in negative language."],["Aurobindo","‘Who is the unseen Power behind the visible activity of the senses?’ — this is the entire question of the Upaniṣad, and the entire question of consciousness. The Kena begins where modern philosophy of mind ends: not with the question whether there is a self, but with the question what kind of being it is whose presence makes the question possible."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.2","title":"The ear of the ear, the mind of mind","sa":["श्रोत्रस्य श्रोत्रं मनसो मनो यद्वाचो ह वाचꣳ स उ प्राणस्य प्राणः ।","चक्षुषश्चक्षुरतिमुच्य धीराः प्रेत्यास्माल्लोकादमृता भवन्ति ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["śrotrasya śrotraṃ manaso mano yad vāco ha vācaṃ sa u prāṇasya prāṇaḥ |","cakṣuṣaś cakṣur atimucya dhīrāḥ pretyāsmāl lokād amṛtā bhavanti || 2 ||"],"words":[["śrotrasya","n. gen. sg.","of the ear"],["śrotram","n. nom. sg.","the (true) ear"],["manasaḥ","n. gen. sg.","of the mind"],["manaḥ","n. nom. sg.","the (true) mind"],["yat","rel. pron. n. nom. sg.","that which"],["vācaḥ","f. gen. sg.","of speech"],["ha","indecl. emph.","indeed"],["vācam","f. acc. sg. (here: the very speech-of-speech)","(is) the speech"],["saḥ u","pron. m. nom. sg. + emph.","He, indeed,"],["prāṇasya prāṇaḥ","m. gen. sg. + m. nom. sg.","is the breath of the breath"],["cakṣuṣaḥ","n. gen. sg.","of the eye"],["cakṣuḥ","n. nom. sg.","the (true) eye"],["ati-mucya","ger. of ati-√muc","(the wise,) having transcended (the senses)"],["dhīrāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the wise"],["pretya","ger. of pra-√i","having departed"],["asmāt lokāt","pron. m. abl. sg. + m. abl. sg.","from this world"],["amṛtāḥ","m. nom. pl.","immortal"],["bhavanti","3 pl. pres. of √bhū","become"]],"lit":"“The ear of the ear, the mind of mind, that which is the speech of speech — He is also the breath of the breath, the eye of the eye. The wise, having transcended (the senses), departing from this world, become immortal.”","para":"The answer to ‘by whom?’: it is the ear behind the ear, the mind behind the mind, the speech behind speech, the breath behind the breath, the eye behind the eye. The wise renounce identification with the outer senses, leave this world, and become immortal.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The genitive-plus-nominative formula (śrotrasya śrotram) is not a doubling but a reversal: the apparent ear is the object, the (true) ear is the Self that enables hearing. This is the Upaniṣadic prototype of the Vedāntic ‘sākṣin’ doctrine: the senses are objects to a witness that is not itself a sense."],["Sivananda","Daily contemplation: hear that you are hearing; see that you are seeing; the ‘that-which-is-aware-of’ is the answer. The verse is the practical instruction lying beneath the metaphysical claim."],["Krishnananda","The reversal-of-genitive (‘ear of the ear’) is the canonical Upaniṣadic move for talking about that which makes objectification possible — and which therefore cannot itself be objectified. The Kena names five faculties; the formula could be extended indefinitely. Any faculty whose ‘of’-relation we know has a ‘of-of-relation’ which is the Self."],["Deussen","Closest parallel: Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.4.18 (‘yad vāco vācaṃ manaso mana uta prāṇasya prāṇaḥ’) — almost verbatim. Either the Kena draws from the BĀU or both draw on a common older formula."],["Aurobindo","The verse is the canonical statement of consciousness as the ground of every act of consciousness. ‘atimucya’ — having transcended — does not mean abandoning the senses; it means knowing them as effects of the One who is their inner condition. Then the senses themselves become organs of the immortal."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3","title":"There the eye goes not, nor speech, nor mind","sa":["न तत्र चक्षुर्गच्छति न वाग्गच्छति नो मनो न विद्मो न विजानीमो यथैतदनुशिष्यात् ।","अन्यदेव तद्विदितादथो अविदितादधि ।","इति शुश्रुम पूर्वेषां ये नस्तद्व्याचचक्षिरे ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["na tatra cakṣur gacchati na vāg gacchati no manaḥ na vidmo na vijānīmo yathaitad anuśiṣyāt |","anyad eva tad viditād atho aviditād adhi |","iti śuśruma pūrveṣāṃ ye nas tad vyācacakṣire || 3 ||"],"words":[["na tatra","neg. + adv.","not there"],["cakṣuḥ","n. nom. sg.","the eye"],["gacchati","3 sg. pres. of √gam","goes"],["vāk","f. nom. sg.","speech"],["no manaḥ","neg. + n. nom. sg.","nor mind"],["na vidmaḥ","neg. + 1 pl. pf. of √vid","we do not know"],["na vijānīmaḥ","neg. + 1 pl. pres. of vi-√jñā","we do not (precisely) cognise"],["yathā","adv./conj.","how / in what way"],["etat","pron. n. acc. sg.","this"],["anu-śiṣyāt","3 sg. opt. of anu-√śās","one might teach"],["anyat eva","pron. n. + emph.","something else, indeed"],["tat","pron. n. nom. sg.","that"],["viditāt","n. abl. sg.","from the known"],["atho","indecl.","and"],["aviditāt adhi","n. abl. sg. + post-position","(also) beyond the unknown"],["iti śuśruma","quotative + 1 pl. pf.","thus we have heard"],["pūrveṣām","m. gen. pl.","of the predecessors"],["ye naḥ","rel. + pron. enclitic","who to us"],["tat vy-ācacakṣire","pron. + 3 pl. pf. ātm. of vi-ā-√cakṣ","expounded that"]],"lit":"“There the eye does not go, nor speech, nor mind. We do not know (how to define It); we do not (precisely) cognise (how) one might teach this. It is other than the known, and (also) beyond the unknown — thus have we heard from the ancients who expounded it to us.”","para":"The eye cannot reach Brahman, nor speech, nor mind. We do not know how to define it; we cannot precisely cognise how one would teach it. It is other than what is known, and it is also other than the not-known — so the ancients have told us.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘anyad eva tad viditād atho aviditād adhi’ — the most-cited single phrase of the Kena. Brahman is not in the class of known things (we cannot grasp it as an object) and not in the class of unknown things either (it is not the absence of a particular object); it is the condition of the very known/unknown distinction."],["Sivananda","When the disciple says ‘I do not yet understand,’ the Upaniṣad answers: not understanding is itself the correct first response. Frustration here is a signal of approach, not of error."],["Krishnananda","Brahman is neither viditam nor aviditam — neither an object nor an absence of objects. It is the third thing, prior to both. This is the Upaniṣadic prototype of the catuṣ-koṭi (four-cornered logic) that Nāgārjuna will later use."],["Deussen","Standard ‘śuśruma’ formula introducing received teaching, as also in Īśā 10 &amp; 13. The Kena marks here, as the Īśā does, that the doctrine is not first-person revelation but received tradition. The ‘viditāt aviditāt adhi’ formula is a Kena-original construction."],["Aurobindo","Brahman is not knowable as an object, but neither is it the absence of objects: it is what makes object-knowledge possible. The verse blocks both the materialist move (‘it is unknown because it is not yet found’) and the agnostic move (‘it is unknown because unknowable’) — there is a third position, prior to the entire field, in which knowing and not-knowing are themselves modes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4","title":"What speech does not utter — that is Brahman","sa":["यद्वाचानभ्युदितं येन वागभ्युद्यते ।","तदेव ब्रह्म त्वं विद्धि नेदं यदिदमुपासते ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["yad vācānabhyuditaṃ yena vāg abhyudyate |","tad eva brahma tvaṃ viddhi nedaṃ yad idam upāsate || 4 ||"],"words":[["yat","rel. pron. n. nom. sg.","that which"],["vācā","f. instr. sg.","by speech"],["an-abhi-uditam","n. nom. sg. pp. of abhi-√vad + neg.","not uttered"],["yena","rel. pron. m./n. instr. sg.","by which"],["vāk","f. nom. sg.","speech"],["abhi-ud-yate","3 sg. pres. pass. of abhi-√vad","is uttered"],["tat eva brahma","pron. n. + emph. + n.","that alone is Brahman"],["tvam viddhi","pron. + 2 sg. impv. of √vid","know thou"],["na idam","neg. + pron. n. nom. sg.","not this"],["yat idam","rel. + pron. n.","what this here"],["upāsate","3 pl. pres. ātm. of upa-ās","(people) worship"]],"lit":"“That which is not uttered by speech, by which speech itself is uttered — know that alone to be Brahman, not this which (people) here worship.”","para":"Brahman is not what is expressed in any utterance, but the very condition by which speech is possible. Know that alone as Brahman, not the deity that is worshipped under the name.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘nedaṃ yad idam upāsate’ — ‘not this which they worship’ — is the polemical closing-line. Whatever deity is being worshipped as ‘Brahman’ in the ritual cult, that is precisely not Brahman. Brahman is the one who makes the worshipping itself possible."],["Sivananda","Sivananda quoted this verse against sectarianism: every name-and-form one worships is not yet the Brahman the Upaniṣad means. The injunction is not to stop worship but to recognise the difference."],["Krishnananda","The four verses 1.4–1.7 form a structured fivefold proof: speech, mind, eye, ear, breath. In each case the same structure: that-which-X-does-not-do, by which X is done, is Brahman, not the X that is worshipped. Cumulatively this leaves the seeker with no faculty to assert ‘I know Brahman’ — which is the doorway to khaṇḍa 2."],["Deussen","The ‘upāsate’ implies a critique of the ritual cult of the Brāhmaṇas (in which the Kena is itself embedded). The Upaniṣadic moment, on Deussen’s reading, is precisely this internal critique: from inside the Sāma-Veda Brāhmaṇa, the Upaniṣad says ‘not this.’"],["Aurobindo","Each of the five verses 1.4–1.8 makes the same point with a different faculty. Aurobindo reads this not as redundancy but as completeness — the Upaniṣad walks deliberately through every cognitive channel and rules each one out as an object-of-Brahman-knowledge, exhausting the field."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5","title":"What mind does not think — that is Brahman","sa":["यन्मनसा न मनुते येनाहुर्मनो मतम् ।","तदेव ब्रह्म त्वं विद्धि नेदं यदिदमुपासते ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["yan manasā na manute yenāhur mano matam |","tad eva brahma tvaṃ viddhi nedaṃ yad idam upāsate || 5 ||"],"words":[["yat","rel. pron. n. nom. sg.","that which"],["manasā","n. instr. sg.","by the mind"],["na manute","neg. + 3 sg. pres. ātm. of √man","does not think (is not thought)"],["yena","rel. pron. instr. sg.","by which"],["āhuḥ","3 pl. pf. of √ah","they have said"],["manaḥ matam","n. nom. + n. nom. sg. pp.","mind (is itself) thought"],["(rest as in 1.4)","—","“—know that as Brahman; not this which they worship.”"]],"lit":"“That which is not thought by the mind, but by which they say mind itself is thought — know that alone to be Brahman, not this which (people) here worship.”","para":"Brahman is not what is conceptualised by mind, but the very condition by which mind itself is conceptualised. Know that as Brahman, not the conceived deity.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘yenāhur mano matam’ — even our use of the word ‘mind’ presupposes a witness that thinks of mind as object. That witness is Brahman."],["Sivananda","The verse is the Upaniṣad’s caution against ‘mind-worship’ in modern dress — the conviction that consciousness IS the brain or IS the cognitive process. ‘What is the consciousness of consciousness?’ is the Kena’s reply."],["Krishnananda","The verse drives the inquiry one step inward from speech (1.4). Speech can be answered by: ‘there is a mind behind it.’ But what is behind the mind? The Upaniṣad answers: the thinker of mind, who is no thought."],["Deussen","Closest parallel: Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.4.19 (‘manasaivānudraṣṭavyaṃ ... mṛtyoḥ sa mṛtyum āpnoti ya iha nāneva paśyati’). The Kena’s formulation is more compressed."],["Aurobindo","Mind is not the witness; mind is itself witnessed. The error of intellectualism is to take the witnessing faculty as if it were the witness. The Kena cuts that error here."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.6","title":"What eye does not see — that is Brahman","sa":["यच्चक्षुषा न पश्यति येन चक्षूꣳषि पश्यति ।","तदेव ब्रह्म त्वं विद्धि नेदं यदिदमुपासते ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["yac cakṣuṣā na paśyati yena cakṣūṃṣi paśyati |","tad eva brahma tvaṃ viddhi nedaṃ yad idam upāsate || 6 ||"],"words":[["yat cakṣuṣā na paśyati","rel. + n. instr. + neg. + 3 sg. pres. of √dṛś","what one does not see with the eye"],["yena cakṣūṃṣi paśyati","rel. instr. + n. acc. pl. + 3 sg. pres.","by which one sees the eyes (themselves)"],["tad eva brahma…","—","(refrain as 1.4–1.5)"]],"lit":"“That which one does not see with the eye, but by which one sees the eyes themselves — know that alone to be Brahman, not this which (people) here worship.”","para":"Brahman is not what the eye sees, but the seer by whom the very eyes are seen as objects. Know that, not the visible deity, as Brahman.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The reflexive construction ‘yena cakṣūṃṣi paśyati’ (‘by which the eyes themselves are seen’) is the most pointed of the five formulas. The eyes can be observed by anatomy or by reflection in a mirror; but the seer who sees the eyes is not himself an eye."],["Sivananda","Sit. Close the eyes. Ask: who is the one for whom there is now darkness? That one is the eye of the eye."],["Krishnananda","Note the plural ‘cakṣūṃṣi’ — eyes — not just ‘the eye’. Krishnananda reads this as the Upaniṣad’s nod to the multiple modes of seeing (physical, mental, intuitive); the Self is the seer of all of them as objects."],["Deussen","Same construction as 1.4–1.5. Deussen treats the five verses as a single hymn with five strophes."],["Aurobindo","The eye is itself an object; whose object is it? The Kena’s answer: the Self’s. To know the Self is therefore not to see something but to be the seer who is, who is no object to anything."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.7","title":"What ear does not hear — that is Brahman","sa":["यच्छ्रोत्रेण न शृणोति येन श्रोत्रमिदं श्रुतम् ।","तदेव ब्रह्म त्वं विद्धि नेदं यदिदमुपासते ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["yac chrotreṇa na śṛṇoti yena śrotram idaṃ śrutam |","tad eva brahma tvaṃ viddhi nedaṃ yad idam upāsate || 7 ||"],"words":[["yat śrotreṇa na śṛṇoti","rel. + n. instr. + neg. + 3 sg. pres. of √śru","what one does not hear with the ear"],["yena śrotram idam śrutam","rel. instr. + n. nom. sg. + pron. + n. nom. sg. pp.","by which this very ear is heard (i.e., is known as ‘ear’)"],["tad eva brahma…","—","(refrain as before)"]],"lit":"“That which one does not hear with the ear, but by which this very ear is heard — know that alone to be Brahman, not this which (people) here worship.”","para":"Brahman is not what the ear hears, but the hearer by whom the ear itself is heard about, perceived, known. Know that, not any audible deity, as Brahman.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘śrotram idam śrutam’ — ‘this ear is heard’ — is grammatically arresting. The ear is here the object of hearing, not the instrument. The Self hears the ear as ‘ear’."],["Sivananda","Listening to one’s own breath, one notices one is the listener, not the breath. So with the ear: the one to whom the ear is given as an object is the Brahman."],["Krishnananda","The Upaniṣad has now exhausted four of the five jñānendriyas (speech, mind, eye, ear). One remains: prāṇa, breath/life. The next verse closes the set."],["Deussen","Same structural verse as 1.4–1.6; Deussen treats it as the penultimate strophe."],["Aurobindo","The hearer of the ear is not in the list of audible things; therefore Brahman is not audible. But not audible is not the same as silent: it is the unsoundable from which sound itself emerges."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.8","title":"What breath does not breathe — that is Brahman","sa":["यत्प्राणेन न प्राणिति येन प्राणः प्रणीयते ।","तदेव ब्रह्म त्वं विद्धि नेदं यदिदमुपासते ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["yat prāṇena na prāṇiti yena prāṇaḥ praṇīyate |","tad eva brahma tvaṃ viddhi nedaṃ yad idam upāsate || 8 ||"],"words":[["yat prāṇena na prāṇiti","rel. + m. instr. + neg. + 3 sg. pres. of √an (irreg.)","what is not breathed by the breath"],["yena prāṇaḥ praṇīyate","rel. instr. + m. nom. + 3 sg. pres. pass. of pra-√nī","by which the breath is led forth"],["tad eva brahma…","—","(refrain as before)"]],"lit":"“That which is not breathed by the breath, but by which the breath itself is led forth — know that alone to be Brahman, not this which (people) here worship.”","para":"Brahman is not what the breath breathes (the air), but the inner principle by which breath itself is impelled. Know that, not the prāṇa-deity, as Brahman.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> Closes the fivefold formula. With breath ruled out, the entire field of jñānendriyas is exhausted. The next khaṇḍa can therefore step back and address the seeker’s mistake of claiming, at this point, that he ‘knows Brahman.’"],["Sivananda","Practical: watch the breath until you find the one who watches. That one is not a breath."],["Krishnananda","Note the verb ‘praṇīyate’ — is led forth, in the passive. Breath is acted upon, not the actor. Whatever is acted upon cannot be the Self."],["Deussen","Closes the unit; the next strophe (khaṇḍa 2) is doctrinally a new movement, not a continuation."],["Aurobindo","Breath is the most intimate of the senses — closer than even sight or thought. The Kena, having ruled it out as well, has left the seeker with no faculty by which to assert ‘I know.’ This is the threshold of khaṇḍa 2."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Khaṇḍa 2 — The double negation: not-known-not-unknown (verse, 5 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 2","title":"The double negation: not-known-not-unknown (verse, 5 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["If khaṇḍa 1 has exhausted the field of faculties, khaṇḍa 2 turns the inquiry on the inquirer. The seeker now claims, having heard 1.4–1.8, that he ‘knows.’ The Upaniṣad answers: that very claim is the index of ignorance. The five mantras of khaṇḍa 2 are then a sustained meditation on what knowing-without-claiming would actually look like."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1","title":"If you think you know it well — you know little indeed","sa":["यदि मन्यसे सुवेदेति दभ्रमेवापि नूनं त्वं वेत्थ ब्रह्मणो रूपम् ।","यदस्य त्वं यदस्य देवेष्वथ नु मीमाꣳस्यमेव ते मन्ये विदितम् ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["yadi manyase suvedeti dabhram evāpi nūnaṃ tvaṃ vettha brahmaṇo rūpam |","yad asya tvaṃ yad asya deveṣv atha nu mīmāṃsyam eva te manye viditam || 1 ||"],"words":[["yadi manyase","conj. + 2 sg. pres. ātm. of √man","if you think"],["su-veda iti","adv. + 1 sg. pf. of √vid + quotative","‘(I) know (it) well’"],["dabhram eva","n. acc. sg. + emph.","a very little, indeed,"],["api nūnam","indecl. + adv.","still surely"],["tvam vettha","pron. + 2 sg. pf. of √vid","you know"],["brahmaṇaḥ rūpam","n. gen. sg. + n. acc. sg.","of Brahman’s form"],["yat asya tvam","rel. n. + pron. gen. + pron. nom.","what of It (is yours / is in you)"],["yat asya deveṣu","rel. + pron. gen. + m. loc. pl.","what of It is in the gods"],["atha nu mīmāṃsyam eva","conj. + emph. + gerundive + emph.","now indeed (it) is to be inquired"],["te manye viditam","pron. dat. + 1 sg. pres. + n. nom. sg. pp.","I think for you it is (yet) to be known"]],"lit":"“If you think ‘I know (Brahman) well’ — surely you know but a little of Brahman’s form. What of It is in you, what of It is in the gods — these are still to be inquired. I think for you it is (yet) to be known.”","para":"The moment you say ‘I have known Brahman well,’ you have known only a fragment. What is Brahman as it is in you, what is Brahman as it is in the gods — these are still open questions. Take it that you do not yet know.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> Spoken by the teacher (the Upaniṣad’s voice) to the disciple who, having heard 1.4–1.8, prematurely concludes ‘now I know.’ The objection is structural, not personal: any time anyone says ‘I know Brahman well,’ the claim itself is the symptom of partial knowledge."],["Sivananda","The single most important verse for the spiritual aspirant to memorise — it disarms intellectual pride at exactly the moment the practice has begun to bear fruit. Sivananda recited it daily as a guardrail."],["Krishnananda","Krishnananda reads ‘yad asya tvam yad asya deveṣu’ as the canonical two-fold ground of inquiry: Brahman as it lives in the individual (adhyātmika) and Brahman as it operates in the cosmic powers (adhidaivika). To know it well is to have integrated both. To claim knowledge before is premature."],["Deussen","Compares with Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.3.6 (‘neti neti’) — the same disciplinary moment: every positive predication must be withdrawn."],["Aurobindo","The verse is the first of the great Upaniṣadic statements of intellectual humility before the ground of consciousness. The seeker is not told that Brahman is unknowable; he is told that the form of his current ‘knowing’ is exactly what blocks him."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2","title":"I do not think I know — nor do I think I do not know","sa":["नाहं मन्ये सुवेदेति नो न वेदेति वेद च ।","यो नस्तद्वेद तद्वेद नो न वेदेति वेद च ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["nāhaṃ manye suvedeti no na vedeti veda ca |","yo nas tad veda tad veda no na vedeti veda ca || 2 ||"],"words":[["na aham manye","neg. + pron. + 1 sg. pres. ātm.","I do not think"],["su-veda iti","adv. + 1 sg. pf. + quot.","‘(I) know (it) well’"],["no na veda iti","neg. + neg. + 1 sg. pf. + quot.","nor (do I think) ‘I do not know’"],["veda ca","1 sg. pf. + conj.","and (I) know"],["yaḥ naḥ","rel. m. nom. + pron. acc.","he among us who"],["tat veda","pron. n. acc. + 1 sg./3 sg. pf.","knows that"],["tat veda","—","(he) knows it"],["no na veda iti","neg. + neg. + 3 sg. pf. + quot.","nor (does he think) ‘I do not know’"],["veda ca","3 sg. pf. + conj.","and (he) knows"]],"lit":"“I do not think ‘I know (Brahman) well’; nor do I think ‘I do not know’; and (yet) I know. He among us who knows It thus, knows It; nor (does he think) ‘I do not know’; and (he) knows.”","para":"I do not claim to know Brahman well; nor do I claim that I do not know it; yet I know. He among us who has this very knowing — neither the affirming claim nor the denying claim — that one knows.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The four-cornered statement: (1) not ‘I know,’ (2) not ‘I do not know,’ (3) and yet I know, (4) which knowing is itself not the assertion ‘I know.’ Śaṅkara reads this as the canonical Upaniṣadic refutation of every positive and every negative cognitive claim about Brahman. The ‘veda ca’ at the end is the indispensable third move: it is not silence, not agnosticism — it is a knowing whose form is not the form of a claim."],["Sivananda","Recite this verse aloud as a meditation in itself; the rhythm carries the four positions through the body. The verse cannot be intellectually understood until it is rhythmically lived."],["Krishnananda","The classical four-cornered logic (catuṣ-koṭi) of the Mādhyamika is anticipated here verbatim: affirmation, negation, both, neither — and Brahman-knowing is the third (or fifth) move, the one prior to the form of cognitive claim. Krishnananda makes this the centerpiece of his Kena exposition."],["Deussen","Deussen compares with Bhagavad-Gītā 6.20–23 (‘yatra caivātmanātmānaṃ paśyann ātmani tuṣyati’) and notes that this is the most philosophically refined verse in the Upaniṣad. Translates ‘veda ca’ as ‘und weiss es doch’ — and yet knows it."],["Aurobindo","Knowing Brahman is not a state we add to ordinary ignorance, nor a denial of ordinary knowing — it is a third thing, prior to the entire affirming/denying axis of mind. The Kena’s ‘nāhaṃ manye … veda ca’ is the most exact phrasing the Upaniṣadic tradition gives this knowing-without-claiming."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.3","title":"To whom unknown, to him known — and conversely","sa":["यस्यामतं तस्य मतं मतं यस्य न वेद सः ।","अविज्ञातं विजानतां विज्ञातमविजानताम् ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["yasyāmataṃ tasya mataṃ mataṃ yasya na veda saḥ |","avijñātaṃ vijānatāṃ vijñātam avijānatām || 3 ||"],"words":[["yasya amatam","rel. m. gen. + n. nom. sg. (pp. with neg.)","to whom (It is) un-thought"],["tasya matam","pron. m. gen. + n. nom. sg. pp.","to him (It is) thought / known"],["matam yasya","n. nom. sg. + rel. m. gen.","(but) to whom (It is) thought / known"],["na veda saḥ","neg. + 3 sg. pf. + pron.","he does not know"],["a-vijñātam","n. nom. sg. pp.","not-known"],["vijānatām","m. gen. pl. pap. of vi-√jñā","of the knowing (ones)"],["vi-jñātam","n. nom. sg. pp.","known"],["a-vijānatām","m. gen. pl. pap. with neg.","of the not-knowing (ones)"]],"lit":"“To whom It is un-thought, by him It is thought; whomever It is thought by, he does not know. (It is) unknown to the knowing, (and) known to the not-knowing.”","para":"Whoever does not think they have grasped Brahman has grasped it; whoever thinks they have grasped it has not. To those who claim to know, it is unknown; to those who do not claim, it is known.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> Śaṅkara’s gloss: ‘amatam’ — unthought — does not mean ‘not held in the mind’ but ‘not turned into an object-of-thought.’ The verse names two epistemic stances and reverses the ordinary scoring: the one who claims, fails; the one who refuses to claim, has."],["Sivananda","Sivananda’s aphoristic version: ‘The proud guru is the one who has not yet seen; the humble disciple is the one to whom the seeing has come.’"],["Krishnananda","The verse is the structural inversion of ordinary knowledge: in conventional epistemology, the claim ‘I know’ is the proof of knowing. In Brahman-knowing the claim is the disproof, and its absence is the proof. This is not a verbal trick — it follows from the fact that Brahman cannot be an object."],["Deussen","The most chiastic verse of the Upaniṣad; structurally A–B / B–A. Deussen flags it as the locus classicus of the doctrine that ātma-jñāna is not propositional knowledge."],["Aurobindo","The verse is not paradox-mongering. It states the precise relationship between cognitive form and the Self: the act of claiming knowledge of the Self IS the constitution of the Self as an object — and thereby the loss of the Self qua subject. To withdraw the claim is to recover the Self qua Self."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.4","title":"Known by awakening — therein is the immortal","sa":["प्रतिबोधविदितं मतममृतत्वꣳ हि विन्दते ।","आत्मना विन्दते वीर्यं विद्यया विन्दतेऽमृतम् ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["pratibodha-viditaṃ matam amṛtatvaṃ hi vindate |","ātmanā vindate vīryaṃ vidyayā vindate ’mṛtam || 4 ||"],"words":[["prati-bodha-viditam","n. nom. sg. (tatpuruṣa)","known in/by every awakening"],["matam","n. nom. sg. pp. of √man","(is) the (right) understanding"],["amṛtatvam","n. acc. sg.","immortality"],["hi","indecl. emph.","indeed"],["vindate","3 sg. pres. ātm. of √vid","(he) finds / obtains"],["ātmanā","m. instr. sg.","by (the) Self"],["vīryam","n. acc. sg.","strength / vitality"],["vidyayā","f. instr. sg.","by knowledge"],["amṛtam","n. acc. sg.","the deathless"]],"lit":"“(Brahman) is known in every awakening — that is the (right) understanding. (Thereby) one finds immortality, indeed. By the Self one finds strength; by knowledge one finds the deathless.”","para":"Brahman is known in each act of waking awareness — that is the right way of knowing. So one finds immortality. By the Self one finds strength; by knowledge one finds the deathless.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘pratibodha-viditam’ — known-by-each-awakening — is the Kena’s great phrase. Every act of cognition contains the witnessing of cognition; that witnessing is Brahman, and is given to the seeker not as a separate fact but as the inner structure of every act of waking. Śaṅkara takes this as the central practical instruction of the Upaniṣad."],["Sivananda","The most practical verse of the Kena: in every act of awareness, notice the awareness. That is the sādhanā. Brahman is not at the end of a long road — it is the road’s ground at every step."],["Krishnananda","Krishnananda makes ‘pratibodha-viditam’ the title of his book on the Kena. It is the Upaniṣad’s single most precise descriptor of the Brahman-experience: not a special state but the recognised structure of every ordinary state."],["Deussen","Translates ‘pratibodha-viditam’ as ‘bei jedem Erwachen erkannt’ — recognised at every waking. Notes the close parallel with Māṇḍūkya 7 (‘śāntaṃ śivam advaitaṃ caturthaṃ manyante sa ātmā’)."],["Aurobindo","The verse is the bridge between the four-cornered formula of 2.2–2.3 and the practical mysticism of 2.5. The knowing-without-claiming of 2.2 has a concrete locus — every act of awareness — and a concrete fruit — vīrya and amṛta. The Kena is not pessimistic about the inner Self’s accessibility."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.5","title":"Known here, there is truth; not known here, great is the loss","sa":["इह चेदवेदीदथ सत्यमस्ति न चेदिहावेदीन्महती विनष्टिः ।","भूतेषु भूतेषु विचित्य धीराः प्रेत्यास्माल्लोकादमृता भवन्ति ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["iha ced avedīd atha satyam asti na ced ihāvedīn mahatī vinaṣṭiḥ |","bhūteṣu bhūteṣu vicitya dhīrāḥ pretyāsmāl lokād amṛtā bhavanti || 5 ||"],"words":[["iha","adv.","here (in this life / world)"],["ced avedīt","conj. + 3 sg. inj. aor. of √vid","if (one) has known"],["atha satyam asti","conj. + n. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","then there is truth (i.e., immortality)"],["na ced","neg. + conj.","if not"],["iha avedīt","adv. + 3 sg. inj. aor.","here (one) has known"],["mahatī vinaṣṭiḥ","f. nom. sg. adj. + f. nom. sg.","great is the loss / destruction"],["bhūteṣu bhūteṣu","n. loc. pl. (reduplicated)","in being after being"],["vi-citya","ger. of vi-√ci","having discerned"],["dhīrāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the wise"],["pretya asmāt lokāt","ger. + m. abl. sg.","having departed from this world"],["amṛtāḥ bhavanti","m. nom. pl. + 3 pl. pres.","become immortal"]],"lit":"“If here (in this life) one has known It — then there is truth (immortality). If here one has not known — great is the loss. Having discerned (It) in being after being, the wise, departing from this world, become immortal.”","para":"If you have realised Brahman in this life — that is the real life. If you have not — great is the destruction. The wise discern it in every being, leave this world, and become immortal.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The closing verse of the verse-half. ‘iha ced avedīt’ — if known here — locates the entire question in this life, not in any post-mortem state. The Upaniṣad’s eschatology is psychological: amṛta is the recognised-in-this-life amṛta, not a postponed reward."],["Sivananda","Use this verse against any tendency to defer the spiritual life. The Kena does not say ‘later you will know’; it says ‘know now or it is lost.’"],["Krishnananda","‘bhūteṣu bhūteṣu vicitya’ — having discerned (Brahman) in being after being — is the locked-in expression of how the Kena’s contemplative vision becomes a way of looking at the world. Every encountered being is a particular instance of the universal witness."],["Deussen","Compares with Kaṭha 2.3.14 (‘yadā sarve pramucyante kāmā ye ’sya hṛdi śritāḥ’). Deussen reads this verse as the transition-formula between khaṇḍa 2 and the prose narrative of khaṇḍa 3."],["Aurobindo","The closing strophe of the verse half ends in the same image as the second mantra of khaṇḍa 1 — the wise departing from this world become immortal. The Upaniṣad ring-binds the verse half. What follows in khaṇḍas 3–4 is no longer doctrinal exposition: it is dramatic demonstration."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Khaṇḍa 3 — The Yakṣa story: Agni, Vāyu, and Indra humbled (prose)","u":[{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 3","title":"The Yakṣa story: Agni, Vāyu, and Indra humbled (prose)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Hybrid layout: pivotal dialogue (the Yakṣa’s challenges; each god’s reply; the Yakṣa’s test-blade of grass) is pada-glossed like a verse. Connective narration is rendered as flowing prose with Devanāgarī and IAST given but no pada-table."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"§ 3.1","title":"Brahman won the victory for the gods","sa":["ब्रह्म ह देवेभ्यो विजिग्ये तस्य ह ब्रह्मणो विजये देवा अमहीयन्त ।","त ऐक्षन्तास्माकमेवायं विजयोऽस्माकमेवायं महिमेति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["brahma ha devebhyo vijigye tasya ha brahmaṇo vijaye devā amahīyanta |","ta aikṣantāsmākam evāyaṃ vijayo ’smākam evāyaṃ mahimeti || 1 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"Brahman won the victory for the gods (over the demons). In that victory of Brahman’s, the gods exulted. They thought: ‘This victory is ours alone! This glory is ours alone!’","para":"","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The opening line is decisive: it was Brahman, not the gods, who won the battle. The gods misattribute the victory to themselves. This misattribution is the cosmic version of the individual seeker’s ‘I know Brahman well’ in 2.1 — the same error in a different scale."],["Sivananda","The Yakṣa story is the Upaniṣad’s dramatised version of ahaṃkāra-correction: every victory you take credit for is the moment to suspect that something other than you achieved it."],["Krishnananda","The gods are not deceived deliberately; they simply fail to notice the source. This is the structural condition of every faculty: it acts, and forgets the one whose activity it is. The Kena’s parable will not punish the gods but only show them what they had failed to see."],["Deussen","Compares with Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.3 (the contest of senses) — same parabolic device of cosmic forces vying for primacy and being humbled by the underlying principle."],["Aurobindo","The gods are the faculties — fire, wind, the king of the senses (Indra/mind). The story enacts the principle of 1.4–1.8: every faculty wins by the unseen Power’s force and habitually mistakes that force as its own."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 3.2","title":"Brahman appeared to them as a Yakṣa","sa":["तद्धैषां विजज्ञौ तेभ्यो ह प्रादुर्बभूव तन्न व्यजानत किमिदं यक्षमिति ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["tad dhaiṣāṃ vijajñau tebhyo ha prādurbabhūva tan na vyajānata kim idaṃ yakṣam iti || 2 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"Brahman knew (their conceit). It appeared to them. They did not recognise it. (They asked among themselves:) ‘What is this Yakṣa (this mysterious being)?’","para":"","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘yakṣa’ here means simply ‘mysterious presence’ — not the later semi-divine being of the Purāṇas. Śaṅkara explicitly glosses: ‘yakṣaṃ pūjyaṃ mahad bhūtam’ — a worshipful, great being. The gods do not recognise the Brahman they have just been won-the-victory-by."],["Sivananda","What appears in front of you that you cannot place is often what you most need to recognise. The Yakṣa-experience is everyday: it is the unfamiliar presence in the recognised situation."],["Krishnananda","‘tat na vyajānata’ — they did not cognise it. The faculties cannot cognise their own source any more than the eye can see itself."],["Deussen","Renders ‘yakṣa’ as ‘mysteriöses Wesen’ — mysterious being. Warns against importing later Yakṣa-iconography (tree-spirits, treasure-keepers) into the Kena’s usage; here the word has its archaic Atharvavedic sense."],["Aurobindo","Brahman appears — that is the structural point. It does not need to be sought; it stands forward. The gods’ failure is not in seeking it and not finding, but in standing before it and not recognising. The Upaniṣad’s diagnosis of ignorance is therefore not absence of object but failure of recognition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 3.3","title":"‘O Jāta-vedas, find out what this Yakṣa is’ (Agni sent)","sa":["तेऽग्निमब्रुवञ्जातवेद एतद्विजानीहि किमेतद्यक्षमिति तथेति ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["te ’gnim abruvañ jātaveda etad vijānīhi kim etad yakṣam iti tatheti || 3 ||"],"words":[["te","pron. m. nom. pl.","they (the gods)"],["agnim abruvan","m. acc. sg. + 3 pl. impf. of √brū","said to Agni"],["jāta-vedaḥ","m. voc. sg.","O Jātaveda (\"knower of beings,\" an epithet of Agni)"],["etat vi-jānīhi","pron. n. + 2 sg. impv. of vi-√jñā","ascertain this"],["kim etat yakṣam iti","pron. n. + pron. + n. + quot.","‘what is this Yakṣa?’"],["tathā iti","adv. + quot.","‘so be it’"]],"lit":"“They said to Agni: ‘O Jātaveda, ascertain this — what is this Yakṣa?’ ‘So be it,’ (he answered).”","para":"The gods commission Agni — the knower-of-all-beings — to investigate the unknown presence. Agni agrees.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> Agni is chosen first because of his epithet jātavedas — ‘knower of all that has come to be.’ The Upaniṣad is constructing a deliberate test: send the deity most associated with knowledge to identify the Yakṣa. He will fail. The failure is therefore not a failure of stupidity but a failure of knowledge-as-such before the inner principle."],["Sivananda","Even the wisest faculty, sent on this errand, returns empty. The lesson is not anti-intellectual but post-intellectual."],["Krishnananda","Agni = the cognitive light (jñāna-agni). The Upaniṣad is using cosmic-Vedic language to mean: the very light of knowledge cannot grasp its own ground."],["Deussen","Notes that the sequence Agni → Vāyu → Indra reflects the standard tri-loka cosmology — earth-fire, mid-region-wind, heaven-Indra — but reorders priority by giving Indra the last and highest position."],["Aurobindo","Agni represents conscious-Force at its most luminous; Vāyu, conscious-Force at its most subtle and mobile; Indra, conscious-Force at its summit. None of the three can grasp the Power of which they are the modes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 3.4","title":"‘Who are you?’ — the Yakṣa challenges Agni","sa":["तदभ्यद्रवत् तमभ्यवदत्कोऽसीत्यग्निर्वा अहमस्मीत्यब्रवीज्जातवेदा वा अहमस्मीति ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["tad abhyadravat tam abhyavadat ko ’sīty agnir vā aham asmīty abravīj jātavedā vā aham asmīti || 4 ||"],"words":[["tat abhyadravat","pron. + 3 sg. impf. of abhi-√dru","(Agni) rushed up to it"],["tam abhyavadat","pron. + 3 sg. impf. of abhi-√vad","(the Yakṣa) spoke to him"],["kaḥ asi iti","pron. + 2 sg. pres. + quot.","‘Who are you?’"],["agniḥ vā aham asmi iti","m. nom. sg. + emph. + pron. + 1 sg. pres. + quot.","‘I am Agni indeed!’"],["abravīt","3 sg. impf. of √brū","(he) said"],["jātavedāḥ vā aham asmi iti","m. nom. sg. + emph. + pron. + 1 sg. pres. + quot.","‘I am Jātaveda indeed!’"]],"lit":"“(Agni) rushed to It. It said to him: ‘Who are you?’ ‘I am Agni indeed; I am Jātaveda indeed,’ he said.”","para":"Agni hurries to the Yakṣa. The Yakṣa asks: ‘And who are you?’ Agni answers with his name and his epithet: I am Agni, I am the knower-of-beings.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> Agni answers by name and credential — the standard reply of any cognising agent. The Yakṣa’s ‘who are you?’ is the very question of khaṇḍa 1.1 (kena?), now turned around: the faculty is asked to account for itself. Its answer is in the conventional vocabulary of identity, which will be tested."],["Sivananda","The Yakṣa’s question is a perfect spiritual question for any introspective sit: who are you? And note: the first answer will always be the conventional one (the name, the credential, the role). The test is whether anything stands when those are taken away."],["Krishnananda","The double-form of Agni’s self-introduction — name plus epithet — is significant: he asserts both his identity and his power. The next exchange will test the power."],["Deussen","The dialogue uses a stock formula: kim asi / kaḥ asi (‘what / who are you?’) is found in similar form in JaiminīyaUpaniṣad-Brāhmaṇa 3.10."],["Aurobindo","Agni names himself by what he is for others (Agni = the fire-god to humans) and by what he does (jātaveda = knower of beings). Neither is what he IS. The Yakṣa is precisely the test of what one IS, prior to function and role."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 3.5","title":"‘What is your power?’ — and Agni cannot burn a blade of grass","sa":["तस्मिꣳस्त्वयि किं वीर्यमित्यपीदꣳ सर्वं दहेयं यदिदं पृथिव्यामिति ॥ ५ ॥","तस्मै तृणं निदधावेतद्दहेति तदुपप्रेयाय सर्वजवेन तन्न शशाक दग्धुं स तत एव निववृते नैतदशकं विज्ञातुं यदेतद्यक्षमिति ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["tasmiṃs tvayi kiṃ vīryam ity apīdaṃ sarvaṃ daheyaṃ yad idaṃ pṛthivyām iti || 5 ||","tasmai tṛṇaṃ nidadhāv etad daheti tad upapreyāya sarva-javena tan na śaśāka dagdhuṃ sa tata eva nivavṛte naitad aśakaṃ vijñātuṃ yad etad yakṣam iti || 6 ||"],"words":[["tasmin tvayi","pron. m. loc. sg. + pron.","‘In you (in such a one as you)’"],["kim vīryam iti","pron. n. + n. nom. + quot.","‘what power (is there)?’"],["api idam sarvam daheyam","emph. + pron. + n. + 1 sg. pot. of √dah","‘(I could) burn even all this’"],["yat idam pṛthivyām","rel. + pron. + f. loc. sg.","‘that is on earth’"],["tasmai tṛṇam nidadhau","pron. dat. + n. acc. + 3 sg. pf. of ni-√dhā","(the Yakṣa) placed a blade of grass before him"],["etat daha iti","pron. + 2 sg. impv. of √dah + quot.","‘burn this!’"],["tat upa-preyāya","pron. + 3 sg. pf. of upa-pra-√i","(Agni) approached it"],["sarva-javena","m. instr. sg.","with all (his) speed"],["tat na śaśāka dagdhum","pron. + neg. + 3 sg. pf. of √śak + inf. of √dah","could not burn it"],["saḥ tataḥ eva nivavṛte","pron. + adv. + emph. + 3 sg. pf. of ni-√vṛt","He turned back from there"],["na etat aśakam vijñātum","neg. + pron. + 1 sg. impf. of √śak + inf.","‘I could not ascertain this’"],["yat etat yakṣam iti","rel. + pron. + n. + quot.","‘what this Yakṣa is’"]],"lit":"“‘In such a one as you, what is the power?’ ‘I could burn all this that is on earth.’ It placed a blade of grass before him: ‘Burn this!’ He approached it with all his speed; he could not burn it. He turned back from there. ‘I could not ascertain what this Yakṣa is.’”","para":"The Yakṣa asks Agni what his power is. Agni boasts: I can burn everything on earth. The Yakṣa lays a blade of grass before him: ‘burn this.’ Agni hurls his full speed at it; the grass does not burn. Defeated, he returns and tells the gods: I could not find out what this is.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The blade of grass is chosen deliberately: the smallest, the easiest. The Upaniṣad is showing that without the inner Brahman the largest power cannot do the smallest act. The failure is total."],["Sivananda","Sivananda used this verse to discipline the spiritual ego: when you cannot, by your own power, accomplish the smallest task, ask who the doer is. Often the small failures are the most revelatory."],["Krishnananda","Agni does not fail through weakness of fire but through the withdrawal of the inner Power. The lesson is precise: any faculty’s power IS its inner Brahman, never its own; the moment the Yakṣa demonstrates that, the faculty has no recourse."],["Deussen","Compares with Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.3.2 (the senses-contest), where each sense in turn boasts its power and is shown weak."],["Aurobindo","Agni’s defeat is not punitive; it is revelatory. He had to come to know that even the burning he could ordinarily do was not his own. The Kena replaces the Vedic hymnal pride in Agni with the Upaniṣadic recognition of Agni-as-effect."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 3.6","title":"‘O Mātariśvan, find out what this Yakṣa is’ (Vāyu sent and fails)","sa":["अथ वायुमब्रुवन्वायवेतद्विजानीहि किमेतद्यक्षमिति तथेति ॥ ७ ॥","तदभ्यद्रवत्तमभ्यवदत्कोऽसीति वायुर्वा अहमस्मीत्यब्रवीन्मातरिश्वा वा अहमस्मीति ॥ ८ ॥","तस्मिꣳस्त्वयि किं वीर्यमित्यपीदꣳ सर्वमाददीय यदिदं पृथिव्यामिति ॥ ९ ॥","तस्मै तृणं निदधावेतदादत्स्वेति तदुपप्रेयाय सर्वजवेन तन्न शशाकादातुम् ।","स तत एव निववृते नैतदशकं विज्ञातुं यदेतद्यक्षमिति ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["atha vāyum abruvan vāyav etad vijānīhi kim etad yakṣam iti tatheti || 7 ||","tad abhyadravat tam abhyavadat ko ’sīti vāyur vā aham asmīty abravīn mātariśvā vā aham asmīti || 8 ||","tasmiṃs tvayi kiṃ vīryam ity apīdaṃ sarvam ādadīya yad idaṃ pṛthivyām iti || 9 ||","tasmai tṛṇaṃ nidadhāv etad ādatsveti tad upapreyāya sarva-javena tan na śaśākādātum |","sa tata eva nivavṛte naitad aśakaṃ vijñātuṃ yad etad yakṣam iti || 10 ||"],"words":[["vāyo","m. voc. sg.","‘O Vāyu’"],["mātariśvā vā aham asmi","m. nom. sg. + emph. + pron. + 1 sg. pres.","‘I am Mātariśvan (\"he-who-moves-in-the-mother\") indeed!’"],["api idam sarvam ādadīya","emph. + pron. + n. + 1 sg. pot. of ā-√dā","‘(I could) seize even all this’"],["etat ādatsva iti","pron. + 2 sg. impv. ātm. of ā-√dā + quot.","‘seize this!’"],["tat na śaśāka ādātum","pron. + neg. + 3 sg. pf. + inf. of ā-√dā","could not seize it"]],"lit":"“Then they said to Vāyu: ‘O Vāyu, ascertain this — what is this Yakṣa?’ ‘So be it,’ (he said). He rushed to It. It said to him: ‘Who are you?’ ‘I am Vāyu indeed; I am Mātariśvan indeed.’ ‘In such a one as you, what is the power?’ ‘I could seize all this that is on earth.’ It placed a blade of grass before him: ‘Seize this!’ He approached it with all his speed; he could not seize it. He turned back from there. ‘I could not ascertain what this Yakṣa is.’”","para":"The gods send Vāyu next. The same dialogue plays out with one word changed: where Agni had ‘burn,’ Vāyu has ‘seize/carry off.’ The Yakṣa places the same blade of grass before him. Vāyu cannot move it. He returns defeated and reports: I could not find out what this is.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> Parallel to 3.5, with ‘ādatsva’ (carry off) replacing ‘daha’ (burn). The Upaniṣad uses verbatim repetition with substitution to underscore the universality of the principle: it is not Agni-specific, not Vāyu-specific — it is the structural condition of every faculty."],["Sivananda","Vāyu boasts of carrying everything off; he cannot lift a blade of grass. The wind is the most ‘subtle’ of the cosmic senses. Even subtlety fails. The progression Agni → Vāyu is the Upaniṣad’s polite ‘subtler powers fail too.’"],["Krishnananda","The repetition is doctrinally important: it shows that there is no faculty, however close to the source (Mātariśvan, ‘the one who moves in the mother’ = primal cosmic motion), that can grasp its own ground."],["Deussen","Same construction as 3.5; Deussen prints them in parallel columns in his Sechzig Upaniṣad’s."],["Aurobindo","Mātariśvan, the cosmic in-mother-mover, is a deeper category than fire — yet equally helpless. The Upaniṣad is exhausting the cosmic forces in order to leave only Indra (the inner king of the senses) and then to leave Indra too."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 3.11–12","title":"Indra approaches; the Yakṣa vanishes; in the same sky a woman appears","sa":["अथेन्द्रमब्रुवन्मघवन्नेतद्विजानीहि किमेतद्यक्षमिति तथेति तदभ्यद्रवत्तस्मात्तिरोदधे ॥ ११ ॥","स तस्मिन्नेवाकाशे स्त्रियमाजगाम बहुशोभमानामुमाꣳ हैमवतीं ताꣳ होवाच किमेतद्यक्षमिति ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["athendram abruvan maghavann etad vijānīhi kim etad yakṣam iti tatheti tad abhyadravat tasmāt tirodadhe || 11 ||","sa tasminn evākāśe striyam ājagāma bahuśobhamānām umāṃ haimavatīṃ tāṃ hovāca kim etad yakṣam iti || 12 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"Then they said to Indra: ‘O Maghavan, ascertain this — what is this Yakṣa?’ ‘So be it,’ (he said). He rushed to It. (But) It vanished from before him. In that very space (ākāśa) he came upon a woman, exceedingly beautiful — Umā Haimavatī. To her he said: ‘What is this Yakṣa?’","para":"","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The Yakṣa vanishes specifically for Indra — not, as for Agni and Vāyu, presenting itself for failed test. The vanishing is the third moment: not refusal, but withdrawal into the space (ākāśa) which is itself the inner ākāśa of the heart. Indra encounters there a feminine principle. Śaṅkara identifies Umā Haimavatī with vidyā — the knowledge that is itself the active principle of revelation."],["Sivananda","Notice the deliberate gendering of the moment: brute power (Agni, Vāyu) fails outright; kingly intelligence (Indra) is led further; and the revealer is feminine. The Kena offers no defence of the ordering; it simply marks that the revelation comes through a different mode."],["Krishnananda","Umā Haimavatī is the personification of vidyā — Brahman-knowing as a manifest principle that can be addressed and inquired of. The shift from a Yakṣa (mysterious presence) to Umā (knowable revealer) marks the transition from the via negativa of khaṇḍas 1–2 to the positive disclosure that closes khaṇḍa 4."],["Deussen","Deussen reads Umā as the Vedic personification of speech (vāc) or wisdom (sāṃkhyā), not yet the Purāṇic consort of Śiva. The Kena’s ‘haimavatī’ is more naturally rendered ‘of golden colour’ than ‘daughter of Himavān.’ This is the earliest occurrence of a clearly identified Goddess-revealer in the Upaniṣadic corpus."],["Aurobindo","Umā is conscious Wisdom in its feminine self-revealing aspect — the Mother of the gods, who alone can name the Power they failed to recognise. The Kena’s gender-symbolism is precise: the masculine cosmic powers cannot grasp Brahman, but Brahman’s own self-disclosure is the feminine principle of wisdom."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Khaṇḍa 4 — Umā Haimavatī, the lightning-glimpse, the Upaniṣad sealed (prose)","u":[{"ref":"§ 4.1","title":"‘It is Brahman’ — Umā speaks","sa":["सा ब्रह्मेति होवाच ब्रह्मणो वा एतद्विजये महीयध्वमिति ततो हैव विदाञ्चकार ब्रह्मेति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["sā brahmeti hovāca brahmaṇo vā etad vijaye mahīyadhvam iti tato haiva vidāñcakāra brahmeti || 1 ||"],"words":[["sā","pron. f. nom. sg.","She"],["brahma iti","n. nom. sg. + quot.","‘(It is) Brahman’"],["ha uvāca","indecl. + 3 sg. pf. of √vac","indeed (she) said"],["brahmaṇaḥ vā etat vijaye","n. gen. sg. + emph. + pron. + m. loc. sg.","‘In Brahman’s victory, indeed,’"],["mahīyadhvam iti","2 pl. impv. ātm. of √mah + quot.","‘exult!’"],["tataḥ ha eva","adv. + emph. + emph.","Then, indeed,"],["vidāñcakāra","3 sg. periphrastic pf. of √vid","(Indra) came to know"],["brahma iti","n. + quot.","‘(It is) Brahman’"]],"lit":"“She said: ‘It is Brahman. In Brahman’s victory, indeed, exult!’ Then he (Indra) came to know: ‘It is Brahman.’”","para":"Umā answers Indra plainly: that was Brahman. It is Brahman’s victory in which you have been rejoicing. Hearing this, Indra finally knows: it is Brahman.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The teaching is the simplest sentence in the Upaniṣad: ‘brahmeti.’ ‘It is Brahman.’ Yet it is the answer to the long question that began at 1.1 (‘kena?’). The journey from ‘by whom?’ to ‘brahmeti’ is the Kena’s arc. The voice that says it is Umā’s — feminine wisdom — not any masculine god’s."],["Sivananda","Note the structure of the moment: the disciple-seeker does not realise by his own search; he is told. The Upaniṣad does not subtract the teacher from the path — it integrates the teacher (Umā) as the mode of disclosure."],["Krishnananda","The verse rebukes any mystical theory that requires non-verbal direct experience as exclusive. ‘sā brahmeti hovāca’ — ‘she said “it is Brahman”’ — verbal disclosure IS, in the Upaniṣad’s view, a legitimate mode of revelation."],["Deussen","Marks the structural close of the narrative arc: the question of 1.1 is answered. What follows in 4.2–4.9 is doctrinal elaboration, not narrative continuation."],["Aurobindo","The naming of Brahman by Umā is itself the disclosure — not as new information but as recognition of what had been operating all along. The Upaniṣad teaches: the Self is not revealed by adding a new fact but by removing the misattribution that hid the already-given fact."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 4.2–3","title":"Therefore these gods excel the other gods","sa":["तस्माद्वा एते देवा अतितरामिवान्यान्देवान्यदग्निर्वायुरिन्द्रस्ते ह्येनन्नेदिष्ठं पस्पर्शुस्ते ह्येनत्प्रथमो विदाञ्चकार ब्रह्मेति ॥ २ ॥","तस्माद्वा इन्द्रोऽतितरामिवान्यान्देवान्स ह्येनन्नेदिष्ठं पस्पर्श स ह्येनत्प्रथमो विदाञ्चकार ब्रह्मेति ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["tasmād vā ete devā atitarām ivānyān devān yad agnir vāyur indras te hy enan nediṣṭhaṃ pasparśus te hy enat prathamo vidāñcakāra brahmeti || 2 ||","tasmād vā indro ’titarām ivānyān devān sa hy enan nediṣṭhaṃ pasparśa sa hy enat prathamo vidāñcakāra brahmeti || 3 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"Therefore these gods — Agni, Vāyu, and Indra — excel the other gods, as it were; for they touched It most closely; for they first came to know ‘It is Brahman.’ And therefore Indra, in particular, excels the other gods, as it were; for he touched It most closely; he was the first to know ‘It is Brahman.’","para":"","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The three gods’ ‘excellence’ is precisely the consequence of their humiliation. Failing first and most acutely, they were the ones who came nearest to recognising Brahman. The Upaniṣad converts defeat-of-the-faculty into the very mode of nearness-to-Brahman."],["Sivananda","Spiritual hierarchy in the Upaniṣadic sense: the one who has most directly experienced the failure of his own power is the one closest to the Source."],["Krishnananda","Indra’s further pre-eminence is doctrinally important: he is the king of the senses (= the mind/intellect), and he is the one who undergoes the further movement of meeting Umā. Mind, when it does not boast, is the doorway."],["Deussen","The repetition of the formula for the three gods and then for Indra alone is a deliberate textual move emphasising Indra’s special role."],["Aurobindo","The three faculties that have most directly encountered the limits of their power are made closest to the Power they failed to grasp. This is the Kena’s doctrine of failure-as-the-doorway."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 4.4","title":"Its teaching: like the lightning","sa":["तस्यैष आदेशो यदेतद्विद्युतो व्यद्युतदा इतीन् न्यमीमिषदा इत्यधिदैवतम् ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["tasyaiṣa ādeśo yad etad vidyuto vyadyutadā itīn nyamīmiṣadā ity adhidaivatam || 4 ||"],"words":[["tasya eṣaḥ ādeśaḥ","pron. gen. + pron. + m. nom. sg.","Its teaching is this —"],["yat etat vidyutaḥ","rel. + pron. + f. abl./gen. sg.","‘in the case of the lightning,’"],["vy-adyuta-ā iti","3 sg. impf. of vi-√dyut + emph. + quot.","‘it has flashed — ah!’"],["(it) nyamīmiṣat ā iti","(3 sg. impf. of ni-√miṣ) + emph. + quot.","‘(in the case of the eye) it has winked — ah!’"],["iti adhidaivatam","quot. + n. nom. sg.","— so far as the cosmic gods (adhidaivata)."]],"lit":"“This is Its teaching: ‘(In the case of the lightning) it has flashed!’ — ‘(in the case of the eye) it has winked!’ — so far as the cosmic gods.”","para":"Brahman is to be grasped as one grasps the lightning-flash: not by sustained gaze, but by the sudden, total revelation that comes in an instant and is gone. So in the cosmic sphere.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘ādeśa’ = upadeśa, ‘instruction.’ The lightning-flash is the Upaniṣad’s technical image for Brahman-cognition: a momentary, complete disclosure that defies sustained attention. Śaṅkara reads this as the Kena’s positive-mystical content, paired with the negative formula of khaṇḍas 1–2."],["Sivananda","In meditation, do not chase the flash; cultivate the readiness. The flash will come of itself, and will be missed by the one who hunted it and recognised by the one who waited."],["Krishnananda","The lightning-image is doctrinally precise: lightning illuminates the entire field at once and is gone before it can be appropriated. So is Brahman-knowing. The image rules out gradual cognitive accumulation as the form of jñāna."],["Deussen","Cites Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.3.6 as the closest parallel: ‘sayatas tāḍid iva’ — like a sudden lightning. The Kena’s formulation is briefer and more dramatic."],["Aurobindo","The Power that the gods failed to grasp as a sustained object becomes available to the seer as a flash — and not by accident but by structural necessity. Brahman is not graspable in the form ‘I now hold this’; it is recognisable in the form ‘that just happened.’"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 4.5","title":"Now within the self: ‘the mind goes towards It as if (it sees it)’","sa":["अथाध्यात्मं यदेतद्गच्छतीव च मनोऽनेन चैतदुपस्मरत्यभीक्ष्णꣳ संकल्पः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["athādhyātmaṃ yad etad gacchatīva ca mano ’nena caitad upasmaraty abhīkṣṇaṃ saṃkalpaḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["atha adhyātmam","conj. + n. nom. sg.","Now, with reference to the self —"],["yat etat","rel. + pron. n.","this which"],["gacchati iva ca manaḥ","3 sg. pres. + indecl. + conj. + n. nom. sg.","the mind goes towards It, as it were"],["anena ca etat upa-smarati","pron. instr. + conj. + pron. + 3 sg. pres. of upa-√smṛ","and by this (mind) one remembers (i.e., recalls) It"],["abhīkṣṇam saṃkalpaḥ","adv. + m. nom. sg.","again and again — (this is) saṃkalpa (intention/resolve)"]],"lit":"“Now, within the self — this Brahman is that towards which the mind seems to go, and by it one repeatedly remembers (It); (this is) saṃkalpa.”","para":"Within yourself: Brahman is the inner ‘there’ that the mind keeps reaching towards, and by the mind one keeps remembering Brahman — this is the structure of resolve.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘gacchatīva’ — the mind goes towards It, as if; the ‘iva’ (as if) is critical, because the mind never actually reaches Brahman as object, but Brahman is the structurally inevitable inner-direction of all mental motion. Śaṅkara reads ‘saṃkalpa’ here as Vedic, not Patañjali’s; intention as the recurrent reaching-toward of the mind."],["Sivananda","In daily life: notice that every act of intention has an inner reference to ‘the thing not yet here.’ That reference is the trace of Brahman in the mind."],["Krishnananda","‘abhīkṣṇam saṃkalpaḥ’ — recurrent resolve — is the very texture of intentional consciousness, and the Upaniṣad locates Brahman in that recurrence. To meditate is not to leave saṃkalpa but to recognise it as Brahman’s inner pull."],["Deussen","Compares with Bhagavad-Gītā 8.6 (‘yaṃ yaṃ vāpi smaran bhāvam’) — the closing-thought doctrine of recurrence."],["Aurobindo","Brahman is the inner-pull of every act of intention — the always-towards of the mind. It cannot be reached as a terminus, but it can be recognised as the direction itself."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 4.6","title":"That is called ‘tad-vanam’ — the Loved — and is to be worshipped as such","sa":["तद्ध तद्वनं नाम तद्वनमित्युपासितव्यं स य एतदेवं वेदाभि हैनꣳ सर्वाणि भूतानि संवाञ्छन्ति ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["tad dha tadvanaṃ nāma tadvanam ity upāsitavyaṃ sa ya etad evaṃ vedābhi hainaṃ sarvāṇi bhūtāni saṃvāñchanti || 6 ||"],"words":[["tat ha tad-vanam nāma","pron. + emph. + n. nom. sg. + indecl.","That is, indeed, called ‘tad-vanam’ (\"the one loved by all\")"],["tad-vanam iti upāsitavyam","n. nom. sg. + quot. + gerundive","as ‘tad-vanam’ it is to be worshipped"],["saḥ yaḥ etat evam veda","pron. + rel. + pron. + adv. + 3 sg. pf. of √vid","He who knows It thus —"],["abhi ha enam sarvāṇi bhūtāni saṃ-vāñchanti","prep. + emph. + pron. acc. + n. + 3 pl. pres. of sam-√vānch","all beings turn longing toward him"]],"lit":"“That, indeed, is called ‘tad-vanam’ — ‘the Loved-by-all’ — and as ‘tad-vanam’ it is to be worshipped. He who knows It thus — toward him all beings turn longing.”","para":"Brahman is named ‘tad-vanam,’ ‘that-loved-by-all.’ It is to be worshipped under that name. The one who knows it so becomes the one all beings long for.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘vanam’ from the root √van (‘to love, to long for’) — not ‘forest.’ Brahman is to be worshipped as ‘the loved by all’ — the inner object of every longing in every being. The fruit of this knowing is that one becomes oneself the loved-by-all, the structural object of universal longing."],["Sivananda","Cultivate the recognition that everyone you meet, at the deepest level, is longing for Brahman; respond to them at that level. This is the Kena’s ground for spiritual love."],["Krishnananda","The doctrine of tad-vanam is one of the Upaniṣad’s least-cited but most beautiful teachings: Brahman is the universal direction of love. Every desire, properly traced, terminates in It."],["Deussen","Long discussion in his commentary: ‘vanam’ from √van (to desire); the Upaniṣad coins a name for Brahman from the root of longing itself. The closest later Vedāntic concept is ānanda — but the Kena’s tad-vanam is more relational: not Brahman-as-bliss but Brahman-as-loved."],["Aurobindo","The tad-vanam doctrine integrates the via-negativa of khaṇḍas 1–2 with the eros of universal longing. Brahman is not only the unknowable-which-makes-knowing-possible; it is also the loved-which-makes-loving-possible. The two are one."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 4.7","title":"‘Teach me the Upaniṣad’ — the close","sa":["उपनिषदं भो ब्रूहीत्युक्ता त उपनिषद् ब्राह्मीं वाव त उपनिषदमब्रूमेति ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["upaniṣadaṃ bho brūhīty uktā ta upaniṣad brāhmīṃ vāva ta upaniṣadam abrūmeti || 7 ||"],"words":[["upaniṣadam bhoḥ brūhi iti","f. acc. sg. + voc. + 2 sg. impv. + quot.","‘Sir, please tell me the Upaniṣad’"],["uktā te upaniṣat","f. nom. sg. pp. of √vac + pron. dat. + f. nom. sg.","‘the Upaniṣad has (now) been told to you’"],["brāhmīm vāva te upaniṣadam abrūma iti","f. acc. sg. + emph. + pron. dat. + f. acc. sg. + 1 pl. impf. of √brū + quot.","‘we have told you the Brahmic Upaniṣad’"]],"lit":"“‘Sir, please tell me the Upaniṣad.’ ‘The Upaniṣad has been told to you. We have indeed told you the Upaniṣad of Brahman.’”","para":"The disciple asks: tell me the Upaniṣad. The teacher answers: the Upaniṣad has just been told — the Brahmic Upaniṣad.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The closing question is a structural mirror of the opening: where 1.1 asks ‘by whom?’, 4.7 asks ‘what is the Upaniṣad?’ The teacher’s reply: there is no further teaching; the Upaniṣad is what has just been said. The Kena is self-sealing."],["Sivananda","Use this verse against the impulse to seek ‘the next teaching.’ Where the Upaniṣad has been heard, it is enough."],["Krishnananda","The disciple’s final question reveals he was still expecting a positive, propositional ‘Upaniṣad’ — a content he could hold. The teacher’s answer is: it has already been given. The form of the teaching IS its content."],["Deussen","Standard Upaniṣadic closing formula; cf. Chāndogya 8.15.1, Praśna 6.8."],["Aurobindo","The Upaniṣad has not been a list of doctrines; it has been the staging of a recognition. The recognition is complete. There is no further information to add."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 4.8","title":"Tapas, restraint, karma — the foundation. Vedas — the limbs. Truth — the abode.","sa":["तस्यै तपो दमः कर्मेति प्रतिष्ठा वेदाः सर्वाङ्गानि सत्यमायतनम् ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["tasyai tapo damaḥ karmeti pratiṣṭhā vedāḥ sarvāṅgāni satyam āyatanam || 8 ||"],"words":[["tasyai","pron. f. dat. sg.","of It (the Upaniṣad)"],["tapaḥ","n. nom. sg.","ascetic intensity"],["damaḥ","m. nom. sg.","restraint (of senses)"],["karma iti","n. + quot.","(right) action"],["pratiṣṭhā","f. nom. sg.","(is the) foundation"],["vedāḥ sarva-aṅgāni","m. nom. pl. + n. nom. pl.","the Vedas are all (its) limbs"],["satyam āyatanam","n. nom. sg. + n. nom. sg.","Truth is (its) abode"]],"lit":"“For It (the Upaniṣad), tapas, dama, and karma are the foundation; the Vedas are all the limbs; Truth is the abode.”","para":"The Upaniṣad’s body is composed thus: ascetic intensity, sense-restraint, and right action are its feet; the Vedas are all its limbs; Truth is the place where it lives.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The closing triad is the integral prescription. ‘tapaḥ’ — the warming-up of inner discipline; ‘damaḥ’ — the restraint of the outward-flowing senses; ‘karma’ — right action. Without these three the Upaniṣadic insight has no body in which to live. The Vedas are the limbs (the supports); satyam is the abode (the inner ground). This is the Kena’s doctrine of embodied wisdom."],["Sivananda","Sivananda made this triad the structural backbone of his Divine Life teaching: ‘serve, love, give, purify, meditate, realise’ — but anchored daily in tapas, dama, karma."],["Krishnananda","The triad is not optional pre-conditions but the form of the realisation itself. Brahman-knowing is not an alternative to discipline; discipline is its skeleton."],["Deussen","Compares with Taittirīya 1.9 (‘ṛtaṃ ca svādhyāya-pravacane ca’) — the locus classicus of the integral discipline. The Kena’s formula is briefer; it omits the negotiating ‘also’ and gives the bare triad."],["Aurobindo","The Kena does not end in inwardness but in integral life. Tapas, dama, karma are the limbs of the Upaniṣad — i.e., they constitute the body in which Brahman-knowing becomes a livable reality. Without them, knowing is not embodied; with them, knowing IS the lived life."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"§ 4.9","title":"He who knows It thus, freed from sin, is established in the boundless Heaven","sa":["यो वा एतामेवं वेदापहत्य पाप्मानमनन्ते स्वर्गे लोके ज्येये प्रतितिष्ठति प्रतितिष्ठति ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["yo vā etām evaṃ vedāpahatya pāpmānam anante svarge loke jyeye pratitiṣṭhati pratitiṣṭhati || 9 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ vā etām evam veda","rel. + emph. + pron. f. + adv. + 3 sg. pf.","He who indeed knows this (Upaniṣad) thus"],["apa-hatya pāpmānam","ger. of apa-√han + m. acc. sg.","having struck off (his) evil"],["anante svarge loke jyeye","m. loc. sg. + m. loc. sg. + m. loc. sg. + m. loc. sg. compar.","in the infinite, heavenly, greater world"],["pratitiṣṭhati pratitiṣṭhati","3 sg. pres. of prati-√sthā (doubled)","he is firmly established — is firmly established"]],"lit":"“He who knows this (Upaniṣad) thus, having struck off evil, is firmly established in the infinite, heavenly, greater world — is firmly established.”","para":"The one who knows this Upaniṣad as it is, having shed his evil, is firmly established in the infinite, heavenly, greater world — firmly established.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The closing-formula verse, paralleled in many Upaniṣads. The doubling of the verb (pratitiṣṭhati pratitiṣṭhati) marks the close of the text — a standard Upaniṣadic device for ‘this is the end.’"],["Sivananda","The verse promises ‘establishment’ — pratiṣṭhā — the same word as in 4.8. The discipline that was the foundation is also the result; the seeker is established in what he stood on."],["Krishnananda","‘ananta svarga loka’ — the infinite, heavenly, greater world — is not a postmortem destination but the integrally-realised present world recognised in its dimension as the heavenly. The Kena closes by gathering its argument: tapas-dama-karma → Brahman-knowing → this-world-recognised-as-heaven."],["Deussen","Standard pratitiṣṭhati-pratitiṣṭhati closing; the doubled verb marks the seal of the text in JaiminīyaUpaniṣad-Brāhmaṇa style."],["Aurobindo","The Upaniṣad does not end in transcendence but in establishment. The seeker is not removed from the world; he is set in it more firmly, freed of the evil that obstructed his integral life."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Visual Representations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Five ASCII diagrams of the Kena’s structural logic. Each captures one section’s argument in spatial form."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 1","title":"The ‘by whom?’ inquiry: tracing the sense-trail backward (mantra 1.1)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>             OUTER FIELD                                  INNER SOURCE\n             (the world)                                  (the unknown)\n                  ▲                                              ▲\n                  │                                              │\n                  │   ◀───── speech is uttered ─────◀── BY WHOM?\n                  │   ◀───── mind descends ────────◀── BY WHOM?\n              the    ◀───── breath proceeds ──────◀── BY WHOM?\n             SENSES  ◀───── eye sees ──────────────◀── BY WHOM?\n                  │   ◀───── ear hears ────────────◀── BY WHOM?\n                  │                                              │\n                  ▼                                              │\n              objects                                            │\n              (āyatana)                                          │\n                                                                  ▼\n                                                  the Upaniṣad&#x27;s KENA?\n                                                  — a question, not yet\n                                                  a name. The next verse\n                                                  will answer with the\n                                                  reversal: śrotrasya\n                                                  śrotraṃ, manaso mano…</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 2","title":"Reversal of the genitive: ‘ear of the ear’ (mantra 1.2)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>    ORDINARY EPISTEMOLOGY                          UPANIṢADIC REVERSAL\n    ──────────────────────                          ───────────────────────\n\n    Subject  ──perceives──▶  Object               (the would-be subject is\n       │                       │                   itself perceived):\n       │ &quot;I am the seer&quot;       │\n       │                       ▼                      ┌─────────────┐\n       │                  &quot;the eye&quot;                   │ The TRUE eye│\n       │                                              │ — the seer  │\n       │                                              │ of the eye  │\n       │  the eye is taken as instrument              └──────┬──────┘\n       │  (Sanskrit: instr. cakṣuṣā)                         │\n                                                             │  sees\n                                                             ▼\n                                                       &quot;the eye&quot;\n                                                       (now object,\n                                                       Sanskrit: gen.\n                                                       cakṣuṣaḥ)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 3","title":"The four-cornered logic of 2.2 (catuṣ-koṭi)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>                          [1]                       [2]\n                  &quot;I know (it) well&quot;        &quot;I do not know&quot;\n                          │                           │\n                          │   ◀── BOTH REJECTED ──▶   │\n                          │                           │\n                          ▼                           ▼\n                          ╳                           ╳\n                          ▲                           ▲\n                          │                           │\n                          │     [3] — yet I KNOW      │\n                          │     (knowing-without-     │\n                          │      claim)               │\n                          │                           │\n                          │     [4] — the knowing of  │\n                          │     [3] is itself not the │\n                          │     form ‘I know’         │\n                          │                           │\n                          └─────────┬─────────────────┘\n                                    │\n                                    ▼\n                          BRAHMAN-KNOWING\n                          (pratibodha-viditam)\n                          — the recognition built\n                            into every act of\n                            ordinary awareness</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 4","title":"The Yakṣa story: faculties humbled, the Mother reveals (khaṇḍa 3–4.1)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>                       ┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n                       │   BRAHMAN won the victory       │   ← 3.1\n                       │   (but the gods didn&#x27;t notice)  │\n                       └──────────────┬──────────────────┘\n                                      │\n                                      │ appears as YAKṢA\n                                      │ (3.2)\n                                      ▼\n              ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐\n              │                                          │\n   AGNI ──▶   │   &quot;burn this blade of grass&quot;             │  cannot burn\n   (3.3-5)    │                                          │\n              │                                          │\n   VĀYU ──▶   │   &quot;seize this blade of grass&quot;            │  cannot lift\n   (3.6-10)   │                                          │\n              │                                          │\n   INDRA ──▶  │   the YAKṢA VANISHES                     │  encounters\n   (3.11-12)  │     ┌─────────────────────────────┐      │  Umā Haimavatī\n              │     │ Umā Haimavatī in ākāśa      │ ◀────┼──────────────\n              │     │                             │      │\n              │     │ &quot;It is BRAHMAN.             │      │\n              │     │  In Brahman&#x27;s victory       │      │\n              │     │  you exulted.&quot;  (4.1)       │      │\n              │     └─────────────────────────────┘      │\n              │                                          │\n              └──────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n              The three failures are not punishment; they are\n              the very mode by which Agni, Vāyu, and Indra come\n              &quot;nearest to Brahman&quot; (4.2-3).</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 5","title":"The lightning-glimpse, the inner pull, and tad-vanam (4.4–4.6)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>             ADHIDAIVATA — cosmic                 ADHYĀTMA — within the self\n             ───────────────────────                ──────────────────────────\n\n                  ⚡                                        ╔══════════╗\n              VIDYUT                                       ║  MIND    ║\n              (lightning)                                  ║          ║\n                  │                                        ║ ─→ saṃkalpa\n                  │ flashes                                ║    ─→ saṃkalpa\n                  │ completely                             ║    ─→ saṃkalpa…\n                  │ in a moment                            ║          ║\n                  ▼                                        ║   ↑ (the always-\n              &quot;it has flashed!&quot;                            ║      towards)\n                                                           ╚════╤═════╝\n                                                                │\n                                                                │\n                                                                ▼\n                                                          ┌─────────────────┐\n                                                          │   TAD-VANAM     │\n                                                          │   &quot;the Loved    │\n                                                          │   by all&quot;       │\n                                                          │                 │\n                                                          │   (4.6: every   │\n                                                          │   being&#x27;s       │\n                                                          │   longing is    │\n                                                          │   Brahman-      │\n                                                          │   directed)     │\n                                                          └─────────────────┘</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical Steps and Observations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Eight practices, each anchored in one phase of the Kena. They are designed for ordinary daily use, not as renunciate retreat instructions; the Kena’s closing triad (tapas, dama, karma) is the householder’s discipline."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"1. The ‘by whom?’ pause (mantra 1.1)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Three times a day — on waking, before the midday meal, and before sleep — pause for one breath and ask: ‘In this present perception, by whom is this happening?’ Do not seek the answer. The question is the practice. Sivananda used to teach this as the ‘three-pause’ method."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. The ear-of-the-ear sit (mantra 1.2)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A daily ten-minute sit. Sit upright. Bring attention to hearing. Listen until you find the one to whom the sounds are given. Do not name; do not visualise; just keep refining the attention from sound → ear → hearer. End when the recognition collapses into stillness."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. The ‘not-this’ purification (mantras 1.4–1.8)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Take one of the five verses per day for a week (speech / mind / eye / ear / breath, then two days for review). With each, examine: what am I tempted to identify Brahman with in this faculty? (Some examples: a chant, a thought, a vision, a sound, the breath itself.) Mentally apply the formula: ‘not this — what is the X of X here?’"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4. The knowing-without-claim watch (mantra 2.2)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Once a day, after any spiritual practice (meditation, reading, satsaṅg), examine your inner posture. If you find ‘that was a good practice; I understood something’ — note the claim, do not deny it, simply add: ‘nāhaṃ manye suvedeti.’ The discipline is to feel the inner posture loosen as the claim is gently dropped."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"5. The Yakṣa-test for one’s own knowledge claims (khaṇḍa 3)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["When you next find yourself confident about some piece of knowledge — about a person, a teaching, a situation — ask: ‘can I burn the blade of grass?’ I.e., can I, by my own self-attributed power, do the smallest concrete operation that follows from what I claim to know? If not, the knowledge is partial and is borrowed."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"6. The pratibodha-vidita practice (mantra 2.4)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Upaniṣad’s most precise practical instruction. In every act of ordinary awareness — opening the door, drinking water, saying a sentence — notice the awareness as the act happens. The aim is not to interrupt the act but to recognise its inner witness. Sivananda’s version: ‘Watch the watcher; not as a second look, but as the structure of the first.’"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"7. The lightning-readiness (mantra 4.4)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Brahman-cognition is a flash, not a sustained gaze. The practice is not to hunt the flash but to make oneself ready: by being settled (āsana), still (mauna), and uncluttered (vairāgya). Then, when the flash comes — in meditation, in a moment of beauty, in a moment of failure — it will be recognised."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"8. The tad-vanam recognition (mantra 4.6)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["In each interaction, before reacting to surface content, recognise: this being, at the deepest level, is longing for Brahman. The Kena’s instruction is not to bypass the surface but to ground the surface in the deeper longing. The fruit, the verse says, is that all beings turn longing toward the one who knows this."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"9. The tapaḥ-damaḥ-karma triad as daily structure (mantra 4.8)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Structure the day so that each of the three is given its place: tapas as the morning sit (or fast, or austere study); dama as a chosen restraint applied through the day (one indulgence relinquished); karma as a chosen act of service or right work. The Upaniṣad does not propose these as advanced disciplines but as the body in which Brahman-knowing becomes livable."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-references in the Upaniṣadic Corpus","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Kena’s closest sibling is the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka (the senses-contest of 1.3 and the antaryāmin-brāhmaṇa of 3.7 are the prose-narrative precedents for the Yakṣa story; ‘yad vāco vācam’ in 4.4.18 is the verbal precedent for Kena 1.2). The Kena is also the Upaniṣadic source most frequently echoed in the Bhagavad-Gītā.","<table><tr><td>Kena passage</td><td>Parallel passage</td><td>Relationship</td></tr><tr><td>Śānti-pāṭha (āpyāyantu mamāṅgāni)</td><td>Chāndogya Upaniṣad — same Sāma-Veda śānti</td><td>Both Sāma-Veda Upaniṣads share this peace-invocation.</td></tr><tr><td>1.1 (kena iṣitam patati preṣitaṃ manaḥ)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 3.7 (antaryāmin-brāhmaṇa, yo ’yaṃ vijñānamayaḥ)</td><td>Both pose the question of the inner controller; the Kena does so as a question, the BĀU as Yājñavalkya’s positive statement.</td></tr><tr><td>1.2 (śrotrasya śrotram manaso mano)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.4.18 (yad vāco vācam manaso mana uta prāṇasya prāṇaḥ)</td><td>Almost verbatim parallel; the Kena has five faculties, the BĀU four. Either Kena draws from BĀU or both from a common source.</td></tr><tr><td>1.3 (na tatra cakṣur gacchati)</td><td>Kaṭha 2.3.12 (na sandṛśe tiṣṭhati rūpam asya); Muṇḍaka 3.1.8 (na cakṣuṣā gṛhyate)</td><td>Three independent statements of the Brahman-cannot-be-seen doctrine.</td></tr><tr><td>1.4–1.8 (refrain ‘nedaṃ yad idam upāsate’)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.4.10 (ahaṃ brahmāsmi); Muṇḍaka 1.1.5</td><td>The polemic against deity-worship-as-Brahman; the Kena’s formula is the most explicit.</td></tr><tr><td>2.1 (yadi manyase suvedeti)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 7.25 (nāhaṃ prakāśaḥ sarvasya)</td><td>The Gītā’s version of the same intellectual-humility doctrine.</td></tr><tr><td>2.2 (nāhaṃ manye suvedeti)</td><td>Māṇḍūkya 7 (śāntaṃ śivam advaitam … sa ātmā sa vijñeyaḥ)</td><td>Both texts circumscribe Brahman-knowing as not-an-object-of-claim.</td></tr><tr><td>2.2 — the four-cornered formula</td><td>Nāgārjuna, Mūla-Madhyamaka-Kārikā 18.8 (sarvaṃ tathyaṃ na vā tathyaṃ)</td><td>The Mādhyamika catuṣ-koṭi is the Buddhist appropriation of the Kena’s structure (with Brahman replaced by śūnyatā).</td></tr><tr><td>2.3 (yasyāmataṃ tasya matam)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 7.25 (continued); Yoga-Sūtra 4.18 (sadā jñātāś citta-vṛttayaḥ)</td><td>Variations on the structural inversion of cognitive claim.</td></tr><tr><td>2.4 (pratibodha-viditam)</td><td>Māṇḍūkya 2 (ayam ātmā brahma) + Māṇḍūkya 7 (turīya)</td><td>The Kena’s ‘known by every awakening’ is the proto-formulation of the turīya-doctrine.</td></tr><tr><td>3.1 (brahma ha devebhyo vijigye)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.3 (the senses-contest with the asuras)</td><td>The Kena’s Yakṣa story is structurally a sequel: the senses won the previous round in BĀU 1.3; now in Kena they must be humbled before their own Source.</td></tr><tr><td>3.3–10 (Agni &amp; Vāyu fail the blade-of-grass test)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.3.2–6 (each sense in turn boasts and is shown weak)</td><td>The parabolic structure (boast → test → failure → return) is shared. The Kena adds the Yakṣa as the testing figure.</td></tr><tr><td>4.1 (sā brahmeti hovāca — Umā)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.4 (Yājñavalkya-Maitreyī dialogue)</td><td>Both stage feminine-mediated Brahman-disclosure; the Kena does so in narrative form, the BĀU in dialogic form.</td></tr><tr><td>4.4 (the lightning teaching)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.3.6 (sayatas tāḍid iva)</td><td>Verbatim parallel of the vidyut-image for Brahman-cognition.</td></tr><tr><td>4.6 (tad-vanam — ‘the Loved-by-all’)</td><td>Taittirīya 2.7 (raso vai saḥ); Chāndogya 8.7.1 (ya ātmā apahatapāpmā)</td><td>Three Upaniṣadic Brahman-by-love-name formulations; the Kena’s tad-vanam is the most explicitly eros-language.</td></tr><tr><td>4.8 (tapo damaḥ karmeti pratiṣṭhā)</td><td>Taittirīya 1.9 (ṛtaṃ ca svādhyāya-pravacane ca); Bhagavad-Gītā 16.1–3</td><td>Standard Upaniṣadic triad for embodied wisdom.</td></tr><tr><td>4.9 (apahatya pāpmānam … pratitiṣṭhati)</td><td>Chāndogya 8.4.1 (sa eṣa setur vidhṛtir eṣāṃ lokānām)</td><td>Closing pratitiṣṭhati-formula; standard Upaniṣadic seal.</td></tr></table>","The most consequential cross-reference: the four-cornered formula of Kena 2.2 (nāhaṃ manye suvedeti no na vedeti veda ca) anticipates by several centuries the catuṣ-koṭi that Nāgārjuna will make the centerpiece of the Mūla-Madhyamaka-Kārikā. Whether Nāgārjuna read the Kena directly is debated; that the logical structure is Kena’s is not."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Sanskrit text","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Talavakāra (Jaiminīya) Brāhmaṇa of the Sāma-Veda, JaiminīyaUpaniṣad-Brāhmaṇa 4.18 ff. — sanskritdocuments.org, ITRANS encoding (file: KenaUpanishad.itx).","Critical text and translation in Olivelle, Patrick (1998), The Early Upaniṣads: Annotated Text and Translation, Oxford University Press.","Limaye, V. P. &amp; R. D. Vadekar (eds.) (1958), Eighteen Principal Upaniṣads, Vol. 1 — diplomatic edition with apparatus."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The five commentaries","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Gambhirananda, Swami (tr.) (1957/repr.), Eight Upaniṣads with the Commentary of Śaṅkarācārya, Vol. 1, Advaita Ashrama, Kolkata — contains Śaṅkara’s Pada-bhāṣya (verse khaṇḍas) and Vākya-bhāṣya (prose khaṇḍas) on the Kena. The only Upaniṣad on which Śaṅkara wrote two separate bhāṣyas.","Sivananda, Swami (1937, repr. 1972), The Principal Upanishads, Divine Life Society, Rishikesh — translation and running commentary on the Kena; supplemented by Sivananda, Kena-Upaniṣad lectures (DLS audio archive).","Krishnananda, Swami (1971), The Kena-Upaniṣad, Divine Life Society, Rishikesh; supplemented by his recorded lectures (DLS Rishikesh audio archive), which emphasise the catuṣ-koṭi structure of 2.2.","Deussen, Paul (1897), Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda aus dem Sanskrit übersetzt, F. A. Brockhaus, Leipzig; English translation: Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda, tr. V. M. Bedekar and G. B. Palsule, Motilal Banarsidass, Delhi, 1980.","Aurobindo, Sri (1914, rev. 1952), Kena Upanishad, Sri Aurobindo Ashram, Pondicherry; in Volume 18 of the Complete Works of Sri Aurobindo (CWSA). The longest commentary Aurobindo wrote on any single Upaniṣad, and one of his most important philosophical works."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Secondary scholarship","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Hume, R. E. (1921, 2nd ed. 1931), The Thirteen Principal Upanishads, Oxford University Press.","Radhakrishnan, S. (1953), The Principal Upaniṣads, George Allen &amp; Unwin, London — extensive notes on the Kena.","Ranade, R. D. (1926), A Constructive Survey of Upaniṣadic Philosophy, Bharatiya Vidya Bhavan, repr. 1968.","Roebuck, Valerie J. (2003), The Upaniṣads, Penguin Classics — accessible literary translation with notes.","Olivelle, Patrick (1998), The Early Upaniṣads: Annotated Text and Translation, Oxford University Press — chapter on Kena treats the verse and prose halves as originally independent.","Coomaraswamy, A. K. (1933), ‘On the One and Only Transmigrant,’ JAOS Suppl. 3 — discusses the Yakṣa as Brahman-as-mysterious-Power."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Sister-Upaniṣads of the Sāma-Veda","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Chāndogya Upaniṣad — the principal Upaniṣad of the Sāma-Veda; shares the śānti-pāṭha; contains the Uddālaka-Śvetaketu dialogues (Ch. 6) and the canonical mahā-vākya ‘tat tvam asi.’","JaiminīyaUpaniṣad-Brāhmaṇa — the host text of the Kena (chs. 4.18 ff.); also contains many short Upaniṣadic prose-pieces parallel to the Kena’s khaṇḍa 3–4 narrative."],"kind":"note"}]}]};
